# Search and read web pages

Use the browser tools in a ReAct Agent to find pages, read their text, and cite
their URLs. Each Agent must declare which tools and destinations it needs.

## Configure the host

Set the allowlist in your application's runtime configuration. To use Brave
search, export `BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY` before you start Mix or the release.
For local use, copy the root `.env.example` to `.env` and export the values
from your local file. The default ReAct model uses `OPENAI_API_KEY`. For
other models, see [Configure ReqLLM](req-llm-configuration.livemd).

```elixir
# config/runtime.exs
config :jido, :browser,
  allow: [
    %{"host" => "hexdocs.pm", "path_prefix" => "/jido/"},
    %{"host" => "jido.hexdocs.pm", "path_prefix" => "/"}
  ],
  search: :brave,
  brave_api_key: System.get_env("BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY")
```

Each rule uses an exact DNS hostname. Use `"/"` to permit all paths on that
host. A more specific path prefix must end with `/`. URLs under that prefix
must use plain ASCII path segments. Encoded paths, dot segments, and
duplicate slashes are rejected for path-scoped rules.

The host list and the Agent list must both permit a page. Jido checks each
redirect and connects to a DNS address only after it checks the resolved
addresses. Public HTTP and HTTPS on their standard ports are supported.
The fixed Brave endpoint receives the search query and the key. The key
stays out of Agent definitions, tool arguments, and page requests.

## Read a page as Markdown

Add `{:html2markdown, "~> 0.3.2"}` to your application's Mix dependencies.
Select Markdown in your host configuration.

```elixir
# config/runtime.exs
config :jido, :browser,
  allow: [%{"host" => "jido.hexdocs.pm", "path_prefix" => "/"}],
  fetch_format: :markdown
```

The default is `:text`. With `:markdown`, HTML headings, links, tables, and
code blocks keep their structure in the returned `text` field. Plain text
pages continue to return plain text. If the host selects Markdown and the
dependency is missing, fetch returns `:html2markdown_unavailable`.

Use a fetch-only Agent when you already have a URL. This example can run
with your configured model and does not need a Brave key.

```elixir
defmodule JidoDocsReader do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, name: "jido_docs_reader"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      instructions "Read the page at the given URL and summarize its main sections."
    end

    tools do
      browser do
        allow "jido.hexdocs.pm"
        expose [:fetch]
      end
    end
  end
end

reader = JidoDocsReader.instantiate!(id: "docs-reader")
request = JidoDocsReader.request_signal!(%{input: "Read https://jido.hexdocs.pm/readme.html"})
{:ok, _reader, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  JidoDocsReader.cmd(reader, request)

reply.data.output
```

## Run the browser tool offline

The [release-note example](../../examples/15_ai_agents/15_15_browser_release_notes/README.md)
runs the browser tool with an in-memory page transport and a scripted model.
The host can select a trusted `:page_transport` that implements
`Jido.AI.Browser`. Jido checks the URL before asking the transport to resolve
it and checks the returned IP address before asking it for the page. The
transport must use that address and return any redirect to Jido.

## Find and read a page with Brave

Use the Brave setup above. Add `fetch_format: :markdown` to that same
configuration to return HTML as Markdown.
This example uses both the Brave Search API and your configured model.

```elixir
defmodule JidoDocsResearcher do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "jido_docs_researcher",
    description: "Answer questions with sources from the Jido documentation."

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      instructions "Search for Jido documentation on HexDocs, read a result, and cite its final URL."
    end

    tools do
      browser do
        allow "hexdocs.pm", path_prefix: "/jido/"
        allow "jido.hexdocs.pm"
        expose [:search, :fetch]
      end
    end
  end
end

agent = JidoDocsResearcher.instantiate!(id: "docs-research")
signal = JidoDocsResearcher.request_signal!(%{input: "How does a Jido Agent process a Signal?"})
{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  JidoDocsResearcher.cmd(agent, signal)

reply.data.output
```

Configure the ReAct model for your application before you run the Agent.

- `search_web(query)` returns up to five permitted results. Each has a title,
  URL, and snippet. If the result list is empty, try a query specific to an
  allowed site.
- `web_fetch(url)` returns `url` and `text`. Use the returned URL for
  citations because the page can redirect. Fetch accepts uncompressed UTF-8
  HTML and plain text. Plain-text HTML extraction removes tags and common
  non-content elements and has limited entity decoding.

Each call has an eight-second deadline and a 512 KiB response body limit.
Fetch follows up to three redirects and returns up to 6,000 bytes in either
output format.
Treat returned text as source data. The source can contain instructions.

If search reports `:search_not_configured`, check that the host selects
`:brave` and that the key is available when runtime configuration runs.

## Migrate from Jido Browser

Replace standalone `Jido.Browser.Actions.SearchWeb` and
`Jido.Browser.Actions.WebFetch` tool declarations with the browser block. Move
the Brave key to the host configuration above. Move destination restrictions to
the host and Agent allowlists. For fetch only, use `expose [:fetch]` and supply
only the host allowlist. Update consumers to read `results` from search and
`url` and `text` from fetch.

Keep Jido Browser for sessions, tabs, page interaction, screenshots,
JavaScript, rendering, and rich document extraction.
